  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "L1-axiom",
      "text": "Axiom [L1]: Docker Compose restart policy is declarative configuration. The statement 'docker compose always restarts failed containers' is treated as an axiom only in the limited sense that it is a configuration contract, not an empirical fact; it depends on the restart policy being set and the Docker daemon running.",
      "layer": "L1"
    },
    {
      "id": "L2-observed",
      "text": "Observed evidence [L2]: In our environment, an event/observability log shows the backend container exited with code 137 (or non-zero) several times and was subsequently restarted by the Docker daemon. The restart is observed directly from container state transitions, but the observation is specific to our deployment pipeline and our Docker version.",
      "layer": "L2"
    },
    {
      "id": "L3-heuristic",
      "text": "Heuristic [L3]: 'The backend is self-healing' is a heuristic inference. Scope: it applies only to process-level crashes (container exit), not to application-level failures (e.g. deadlock, memory leak, partial state corruption, misconfigured health check, or the container running but serving 500s). Counterexample: a backend that is alive but in a corrupt state will not be restarted by the compose restart policy; the heuristic fails. Another counterexample: if the restart policy is set to 'no' (Compose default is actually 'no' unless explicitly defined), no restart happens at all, so the statement is false outside the scoped configuration.",
      "layer": "L3"
    },
    {
      "id": "conclusion-confidence-cap",
      "text": "Core conclusion [L4 conclusion, with confidence cap]: 'Our backend is self-healing via docker compose' is supported only up to the confidence of the weakest honest layer, which is the L3 heuristic. Therefore the confidence ceiling is: moderate/high for process-level crash recovery in our configured environment, and low/zero for overall backend health. The claim cannot be promoted to a general law (L5) because it depends on environment-specific configuration and ignores application-level failure modes.",
      "layer": "L4"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": "compose-file-default",
      "kind": "documentation-reference — Compose restart policy defaults to 'no' when unspecified; 'always' restarts regardless of exit code, 'on-failure' restarts only on non-zero exit"
    },
    {
      "id": "observability-log",
      "kind": "empirical-log — our container exit/restart events from the deployment environment"
    },
    {
      "id": "failure-mode-analysis",
      "kind": "theoretical-counterexample — container alive but logically corrupted, health check passing but serving errors"
    }
  ],
  "confidence_cap": "L3 — the conclusion is capped at heuristic confidence because the general statement relies on default configuration assumptions and excludes application-level failure modes. Without verifiable evidence of actual crash-recovery events in the current environment, the confidence cap stays at L3.",
  "gaps": [
    {
      "id": "G1",
      "text": "No proof that the restart policy is literally 'always' in the actual production compose file; if it is 'on-failure' or not set at all, the original statement is factually wrong."
    },
    {
      "id": "G2",
      "text": "No evidence about application-level health: whether the backend returns correct responses after restart, whether state is re-initialized correctly, whether data corruption is detected."
    },
    {
      "id": "G3",
      "text": "No evidence about restart loops or crash loops: if the container exits repeatedly, compose 'always' will restart it indefinitely, which can be misread as self-healing when it is actually a degraded state."
    },
    {
      "id": "G4",
      "text": "The original statement did not include scope/limitations; it asserted 'always' without specifying environment, Docker version, or failure mode."
    }
  ],
  "recommendation": "Replace the statement 'docker compose always restarts failed containers, so our backend is self-healing' with the layered statement: (L1 axiom) compose restart policy is configuration-dependent; (L2 observed) in our env we observed N restarts after process exits; (L3 heuristic) this yields self-healing only for process crashes, not for corrupt state or crash loops; (conclusion) confidence is capped at heuristic level. Fix the declared defect by: 1) explicitly setting restart: always (or on-failure) in the compose file and verifying it in CI; 2) adding a healthcheck that exercises real backend behavior, not just TCP liveness; 3) adding an alert on restart count per time window to detect crash loops; 4) defining migration impact: if restart policy is changed, expect container downtime until manual intervention, so document rollback (revert compose change and re-deploy); 5) acceptance criteria: in a staging test, kill the backend process and assert container state returns to running within X seconds, and assert the healthcheck returns 200 afterward; additionally, in an induced-corruption test, assert the healthcheck fails (proving the heuristic boundary).",
  "three_part_structure": {
    "core_conclusion": "Core Conclusion: The claim is valid only at heuristic level (L3) for process-crash recovery; it is not an empirical law and fails for application-level failures or when the restart policy is unspecified/default.",
    "epistemic_breakdown": "Epistemic Breakdown: L1 axiom = restart policy is a declarative config contract; L2 observed = our environment actually shows container restarts after non-zero exits; L3 heuristic = 'self-healing' holds only for process-level exits, with explicit scope and counterexamples; L4 conclusion capped at L3.",
    "evidence_gaps": "Evidence Gaps: no proof the config is literally 'always' in production; no evidence of post-restart application health; no detection of crash loops; no scope stated in the original claim."
  }
}
